Control flow decides which statements run and how many times. C++'s core constructs mirror C closely.

## Conditionals

```cpp
int score = 85;
if (score >= 90) {
    cout << "A" << endl;
} else if (score >= 80) {
    cout << "B" << endl;
} else {
    cout << "C or below" << endl;
}
```

Any expression convertible to `bool` can drive an `if`: nonzero numbers and non-null pointers are "truthy," zero and `nullptr` are "falsy."

## The Three Loops

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) cout << i << " ";      // counted loop
cout << endl;

int n = 5;
while (n > 0) { cout << n << " "; n--; }           // condition-first loop
cout << endl;

int m = 0;
do { cout << m << " "; m++; } while (m < 3);       // body runs at least once
cout << endl;
```

`for` is best when you know the iteration count or index pattern up front. `while` is best for "keep going until some condition changes," where the condition is checked before each iteration. `do-while` is the only loop that guarantees the body executes at least once, because the condition is checked *after* the first pass — useful for menu-driven programs that must show the menu once before checking whether to repeat.

## The Range-Based `for` Loop

Since C++11, you rarely need manual indices to iterate a container:

```cpp
#include <vector>
vector<int> nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
for (auto& n : nums) {
    if (n % 2 == 0) continue;  // skip even numbers
    cout << n << " ";
}
```

`continue` skips to the next iteration; `break` exits the loop entirely. Both work identically inside `for`, `while`, and `do-while`.

## switch

```cpp
int day = 3;
switch (day) {
    case 1: cout << "Mon"; break;
    case 2: cout << "Tue"; break;
    case 3: cout << "Wed"; break;
    default: cout << "Unknown";
}
```

`switch` compares an integral (or enum) value against a list of constant `case` labels — it cannot switch on a `string` or floating-point value directly.

## for Is Sugar for while

A `for` loop is defined entirely in terms of a `while` loop; the compiler treats:

```cpp
for (init; condition; update) {
    body;
}
```

as exactly equivalent to:

```cpp
{
    init;
    while (condition) {
        body;
        update;
    }
}
```

This explains a subtlety: `update` runs *after* the body, and `continue` inside the body jumps to `update`, not straight back to `condition`. It also explains why a variable declared in `init` (e.g., `int i`) is scoped only to the loop — it lives in that outer `{}` block the compiler introduces around the whole loop.

## Range-Based for Desugars to Iterators

```cpp
for (auto& n : nums) { ... }
```

is roughly equivalent to:

```cpp
for (auto it = nums.begin(); it != nums.end(); ++it) {
    auto& n = *it;
    ...
}
```

This matters for two reasons. First, it's why range-based `for` works uniformly across `vector`, `string`, `map`, and even C-style arrays — anything exposing `begin()`/`end()` (or array bounds) qualifies. Second, it means **modifying the container's size while iterating it invalidates the loop** (the same iterator-invalidation rules discussed in the Arrays/STL module apply), since `end()` may change mid-loop or the underlying storage may move.

## Reference vs. Copy in Range-Based for

```cpp
for (auto n : nums)   { n *= 2; }  // n is a COPY — nums is unchanged
for (auto& n : nums)  { n *= 2; }  // n is a REFERENCE — nums IS modified
for (const auto& n : nums) { ... } // read-only, no copy — best for large elements
```

Choosing `auto` instead of `auto&` for a loop variable is a very common source of "why didn't my modifications stick" bugs, especially with vectors of large objects (strings, custom structs) where it also silently costs a copy per iteration.

## switch Compiles to a Jump Table (Sometimes)

For a dense range of `case` values, compilers often generate a **jump table** — an array of code addresses indexed directly by the switch value — making the dispatch O(1) regardless of how many cases exist, unlike a long `if/else if` chain which is O(n). This is one reason `switch` can be meaningfully faster than an equivalent `if` chain when there are many cases, though modern compilers can sometimes optimize both similarly.

## Missing break Causes Fallthrough

Unlike some newer languages, `switch` cases in C++ **fall through** by default — execution continues into the next case unless a `break` (or `return`) stops it:

```cpp
int x = 1;
switch (x) {
    case 1: cout << "one "; // falls through!
    case 2: cout << "two "; break;
    default: cout << "other";
}
// prints "one two " — case 1 has no break
```

Fallthrough is occasionally used intentionally (e.g., grouping several cases that share behavior), and since C++17 you can silence the compiler warning about it with the `[[fallthrough]];` attribute to document that it's deliberate. Everywhere else, a missing `break` is a bug.

## Off-by-One Errors

The most common loop bug in any C-family language: mixing up `<` and `<=` at a boundary.

```cpp
int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
for (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++) {   // BUG: should be i < 5
    cout << arr[i] << " ";       // reads arr[5], out of bounds — undefined behavior
}
```

C++ performs **no automatic bounds checking** on raw arrays or `vector::operator[]`. Reading or writing one past the end doesn't throw an exception — it's undefined behavior that might crash, might silently corrupt unrelated memory, or might appear to "work" until it doesn't.

## Infinite Loops

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 10; ) {  // forgot the update!
    cout << i << " ";
    // i never changes -> loops forever
}
```

Always double-check that every loop's controlling variable is actually modified somewhere reachable in the body, and that the modification moves the condition toward becoming false.

## Modifying a Container While Range-Iterating It

```cpp
vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};
for (auto& n : v) {
    if (n == 2) v.push_back(99); // undefined behavior: may invalidate iterators
}
```

`push_back` can force the vector to reallocate its internal buffer, which invalidates every existing iterator (including the one the range-`for` loop is using internally) — a crash or silent corruption can follow. If you need to add or remove elements while iterating, either collect the changes in a separate container first, or use explicit index-based iteration with care, or use the erase-remove idiom covered in the STL module.

This program classifies numbers 1 through 30 using a `for` loop, `if`/`else`, `switch`, and both `continue` and `break`, tying the whole module together.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    for (int i = 1; i <= 30; i++) {
        // Skip anything not divisible by 3 or 5 (FizzBuzz-style filter).
        if (i % 3 != 0 && i % 5 != 0) continue;

        if (i % 15 == 0) {
            cout << i << ": FizzBuzz" << endl;
        } else if (i % 3 == 0) {
            cout << i << ": Fizz" << endl;
        } else {
            cout << i << ": Buzz" << endl;
        }

        // Stop early once we've printed our 10th qualifying number.
        static int printedCount = 0;
        printedCount++;
        if (printedCount >= 10) {
            cout << "Reached 10 results, stopping early." << endl;
            break;
        }
    }

    // A menu using switch, run at least once via do-while.
    int choice = 0;
    do {
        cout << "\n1) Fizz  2) Buzz  3) Quit\nChoice: ";
        cin >> choice;
        switch (choice) {
            case 1: cout << "Fizz selected" << endl; break;
            case 2: cout << "Buzz selected" << endl; break;
            case 3: cout << "Goodbye" << endl; break;
            default: cout << "Unknown option" << endl;
        }
    } while (choice != 3);

    return 0;
}
```

The `continue` at the top skips numbers not divisible by 3 or 5 without indenting the rest of the loop body an extra level. The `static int printedCount` retains its value across loop iterations (a `static` local is initialized once and persists for the lifetime of the program), letting the loop count how many qualifying numbers it has printed and `break` out early once it hits ten. The `do-while` menu loop guarantees the menu is shown at least once, and `switch` cleanly dispatches on the numeric choice, with `break` in every case preventing fallthrough.

## Recap

- `if/else if/else`, `for`, `while`, `do-while`, and `switch` mirror C's control flow.
- `for` desugars to a `while` with an init and update step; range-based `for` desugars to iterator calls.
- Use `auto&` (not `auto`) in range-based `for` when you need to modify container elements in place.
- `switch` falls through between cases unless each case ends in `break`; use `[[fallthrough]];` to document intentional fallthrough.
- Raw arrays and `vector::operator[]` have no automatic bounds checking — off-by-one errors are undefined behavior, not exceptions.
- Never resize a container while range-iterating over it; that can invalidate the loop's internal iterators.

codeExamples.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    vector<int> nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    for (auto& n : nums) {
        if (n % 2 == 0) continue;
        cout << n << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;

    int day = 3;
    switch (day) {
        case 1: cout << "Mon"; break;
        case 2: cout << "Tue"; break;
        case 3: cout << "Wed"; break;
        default: cout << "Unknown";
    }
    return 0;
}

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int choice;
    do {
        cout << "Enter 0 to stop: ";
        cin >> choice;
    } while (choice != 0);
    cout << "Done." << endl;
    return 0;
}

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    vector<int> nums = {1, 2, 3};
    for (auto n : nums) n *= 10;        // copy: nums unchanged
    for (auto& n : nums) n *= 10;       // reference: nums IS modified
    for (auto n : nums) cout << n << " "; // 100 200 300
    return 0;
}

