pages.markdown:
A function bundles a reusable chunk of behavior behind a name, a return type, and a parameter list.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int square(int n) {
    return n * n;
}

int main() {
    cout << square(5) << endl; // 25
    return 0;
}
```

## Three Ways to Pass Arguments

C++ gives you explicit control over whether a function works on a copy of the caller's data or the original:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void incByValue(int x) { x++; }        // copies x; caller unaffected
void incByRef(int& x) { x++; }         // aliases the caller's variable
void incByPointer(int* x) { (*x)++; }  // same effect, via an address

int main() {
    int a = 5;
    incByValue(a);
    cout << a << endl; // 5, unchanged

    incByRef(a);
    cout << a << endl; // 6, changed

    incByPointer(&a);
    cout << a << endl; // 7, changed
    return 0;
}
```

**Pass-by-value** (the default) copies the argument — safe, but costly for large objects like big `string`s or `vector`s. **Pass-by-reference** (`Type&`) lets the function operate directly on the caller's variable without copying and without pointer syntax at the call site. **Pass-by-pointer** (`Type*`) achieves similar mutation but requires an explicit `&` at the call site and `*` to dereference inside the function, and it can be `nullptr`, so the function must be prepared to handle that.

## const References Avoid Copies Without Allowing Mutation

For large objects you only need to *read*, the idiomatic parameter type is `const Type&`:

```cpp
double average(const vector<int>& nums) {
    double sum = 0;
    for (int n : nums) sum += n;
    return sum / nums.size();
}
```

This avoids copying the entire vector on every call while still preventing the function from modifying the caller's data — the compiler enforces the `const` promise.

## Function Overloading

C++ allows multiple functions with the same name as long as their parameter lists differ:

```cpp
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
```

The compiler picks the best match based on the argument types at each call site — this is resolved entirely at compile time, unlike the runtime polymorphism covered in the OOP module.

## Every Call Gets a Stack Frame

Each function call allocates a **stack frame** — a region of the call stack holding that call's local variables, parameters, and return address. When `square(5)` is called, a new frame is pushed with its own copy of `n`; when it returns, that frame is popped and its memory reclaimed automatically. This is why local variables and by-value parameters are safe to use inside a function and automatically vanish when it returns — but it's also why returning a pointer or reference to a local variable is dangerous (covered on the next page).

Recursion is the same mechanism repeated: each recursive call gets its *own* stack frame, which is what lets `factorial(5)` call `factorial(4)` without the two invocations' local variables colliding.

```cpp
long long factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return n * factorial(n - 1); // each call has its own n, its own frame
}
```

Because stack space is finite, very deep recursion (tens of thousands of frames, depending on frame size and platform) causes a **stack overflow** — a crash, not a catchable exception. Deeply recursive algorithms on large inputs are often better rewritten iteratively or with an explicit data structure standing in for the call stack.

## Default Arguments

A parameter can have a default value, used when the caller omits it:

```cpp
double power(double base, int exponent = 2) {
    double result = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < exponent; i++) result *= base;
    return result;
}

power(3);     // 9  (exponent defaults to 2)
power(3, 3);  // 27
```

Default arguments must be trailing — once one parameter has a default, every parameter after it must too.

## Overload Resolution

When multiple overloads could match a call, the compiler ranks candidates by how "exact" the match is: an exact type match wins over one requiring promotion (e.g. `int` to `double`), which wins over one requiring a user-defined conversion. If no single overload is strictly the best match, the compiler reports an **ambiguous call** error rather than guessing.

## inline as a Hint

Marking a small, frequently called function `inline` suggests to the compiler that it may paste the function's body directly at each call site instead of generating a call, avoiding call overhead. Modern compilers largely make this decision themselves based on optimization analysis regardless of the keyword; `inline`'s more load-bearing modern purpose is actually allowing a function to be defined identically in multiple translation units (e.g., in a header included by several `.cpp` files) without violating the one-definition rule.

## Scope

A variable is only visible within the block `{}` where it's declared, plus any nested blocks:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int x = 10;
    {
        int y = 20;
        cout << x + y << endl; // both visible here
    }
    // y is out of scope here; using it would be a compile error
    cout << x << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

Function parameters and locals are scoped to the function body; they don't exist before the call and don't persist after it returns (unless declared `static`, which gives a local variable a lifetime spanning the whole program while keeping its *name* scoped to the function).

## Never Return a Reference or Pointer to a Local

```cpp
int& badIdea() {
    int local = 42;
    return local; // DANGER: local's stack frame is destroyed on return
}
// Calling badIdea() and using the result is undefined behavior —
// the reference "dangles," pointing at memory that's no longer valid.
```

The stack frame holding `local` is popped the instant `badIdea()` returns, so the reference (or a pointer, `int* badIdea()`) refers to memory that may be immediately overwritten by the next function call. Good compilers warn about this, but it remains a classic beginner mistake. The fix is to return by value (letting move semantics make it cheap, see the STL module) or to have the caller pass in storage for the function to fill via a reference parameter.

## A First Look at Templates

Writing `int add(int, int)` and `double add(double, double)` separately duplicates logic. **Templates** let you write the logic once, generic over the type:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

template <typename T>
T add(T a, T b) {
    return a + b;
}

int main() {
    cout << add(3, 4) << endl;       // T deduced as int -> 7
    cout << add(2.5, 1.5) << endl;   // T deduced as double -> 4.0
    return 0;
}
```

The compiler generates a separate concrete function for each `T` it's actually called with — this happens at compile time, so there's no runtime overhead compared to writing the overloads by hand. Templates are the backbone of the STL (`vector<T>`, `sort`, etc.) and get a fuller treatment in the context of generic classes in the OOP module, but recognizing `template <typename T>` as "this function/class works with any type" is essential vocabulary from here on.

This program uses value parameters, reference parameters, a `const&` parameter, a default argument, and recursion together.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

// Pass-by-value: safe to use freely, doesn't touch the caller's data.
long long factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return static_cast<long long>(n) * factorial(n - 1);
}

// Pass-by-reference: modifies the caller's variables directly, no return needed.
void minMax(const vector<int>& nums, int& outMin, int& outMax) {
    outMin = nums[0];
    outMax = nums[0];
    for (int n : nums) {
        if (n < outMin) outMin = n;
        if (n > outMax) outMax = n;
    }
}

// Default argument: caller can omit the base.
long long power(long long value, int exponent, long long base = 1) {
    if (exponent == 0) return base;
    return power(value, exponent - 1, base * value);
}

int main() {
    cout << "5! = " << factorial(5) << endl;

    vector<int> scores = {88, 92, 79, 95, 84};
    int lo, hi;
    minMax(scores, lo, hi); // lo and hi are filled in by reference
    cout << "min=" << lo << " max=" << hi << endl;

    cout << "2^10 = " << power(2, 10) << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

`factorial` recurses using pass-by-value, so each call's `n` is independent — no aliasing issues. `minMax` demonstrates the classic C++ pattern of "returning multiple values" via reference out-parameters: instead of bundling `lo`/`hi` into a struct or pair, the function writes directly into the caller's variables. `power` shows a default argument (`base = 1`) combined with recursion, computing `value^exponent` via repeated multiplication without needing a separate helper for the initial call.

## Recap

- Pass-by-value copies the argument; the caller's variable is untouched.
- Pass-by-reference (`Type&`) lets a function modify the caller's variable directly; pass-by-pointer (`Type*`) does similarly but needs explicit dereferencing and can be null.
- Prefer `const Type&` for large objects you don't need to modify, to avoid copies while keeping the "read-only" promise.
- Every call gets its own stack frame; returning a reference/pointer to a local variable produces a dangling reference — undefined behavior.
- Default arguments must trail required ones; overload resolution picks the best match at compile time.
- `template <typename T>` lets one function body work across multiple types, generated per-type at compile time with no runtime cost.

