## vector's Capacity vs. Size

A `vector` maintains two numbers: `size()` (how many elements it currently holds) and `capacity()` (how much space is currently allocated before it must reallocate). When `push_back` would exceed capacity, the vector allocates a **new, larger** buffer (typically doubling), copies or moves every existing element into it, and frees the old buffer:

```cpp
vector<int> v;
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    v.push_back(i);
    cout << "size=" << v.size() << " capacity=" << v.capacity() << endl;
}
```

This growth strategy gives `push_back` **amortized O(1)** time — most calls are cheap, and the occasional expensive reallocation is rare enough that the *average* cost per call stays constant. If you know roughly how many elements you'll store, calling `v.reserve(n)` up front avoids repeated reallocations entirely.

## Iterators Are Invalidated by Reallocation

The reallocation behavior has a sharp edge: any iterator, pointer, or reference into a vector's elements becomes **invalid** the moment the vector reallocates, because the old buffer is freed.

```cpp
vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};
int& ref = v[0];
v.push_back(4);   // may reallocate!
// ref may now dangle, depending on whether reallocation happened
```

This is why holding onto raw pointers/references/iterators into a vector across a `push_back`/`insert`/`erase` call is dangerous unless you know for certain no reallocation occurred (e.g., you called `reserve` with enough headroom beforehand).

## Small String Optimization (SSO)

Most `std::string` implementations avoid heap allocation entirely for short strings (commonly up to 15-22 characters, depending on the standard library implementation) by storing the characters directly inside the `string` object itself, in a small fixed-size buffer. Only when a string grows beyond that inline capacity does it allocate a heap buffer, transparently, with no change to the API. This is why creating and copying lots of short strings is often much cheaper than intuition from other languages suggests — no allocator call is involved at all for most short identifiers, words, or short messages.

## map and set: Associative Containers

Beyond sequence containers, the STL provides associative ones:

```cpp
#include <map>
#include <set>
map<string, int> ages = {{"Ada", 36}, {"Alan", 41}};
ages["Grace"] = 85;              // insert or update
cout << ages["Ada"] << endl;     // 36

set<int> unique = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5}; // duplicates dropped: {1,3,4,5}
```

`map` keeps keys in sorted order internally (typically a balanced tree), giving O(log n) lookup/insert; `unordered_map` (hash-table based) trades that ordering for average O(1) operations.

This program combines `vector`, `string`, `map`, and `<algorithm>` to count and report word frequencies — a small but realistic use of the STL end to end.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <map>
#include <algorithm>
#include <sstream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string text = "the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog the fox runs";

    // Split into words using a stringstream.
    vector<string> words;
    istringstream stream(text);
    string word;
    while (stream >> word) {
        words.push_back(word);
    }

    // Count frequencies with a map.
    map<string, int> frequency;
    for (const string& w : words) {
        frequency[w]++;
    }

    // Copy into a vector of pairs so we can sort by count.
    vector<pair<string, int>> ranked(frequency.begin(), frequency.end());
    sort(ranked.begin(), ranked.end(),
         [](const pair<string, int>& a, const pair<string, int>& b) {
             return a.second > b.second; // descending by count
         });

    cout << "Word frequencies (most common first):" << endl;
    for (const auto& entry : ranked) {
        cout << entry.first << ": " << entry.second << endl;
    }

    // Filter out words that appear only once, using erase-remove.
    ranked.erase(
        remove_if(ranked.begin(), ranked.end(),
                  [](const pair<string, int>& p) { return p.second == 1; }),
        ranked.end());

    cout << "\nWords appearing more than once: " << ranked.size() << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

`istringstream` treats the `string` as an input stream, so `stream >> word` splits on whitespace exactly like `cin >>` would. The `map<string, int>` naturally deduplicates and counts words in one pass — `frequency[w]++` inserts a new entry initialized to `0` on first sight (a feature of `map`'s `operator[]`) and increments it thereafter. Copying into a `vector<pair<string,int>>` is necessary because `map` itself is always sorted by key, not by value, so achieving a "most frequent first" order requires an explicit `sort` with a custom comparator (a **lambda**, `[](...){ ... }`, an anonymous inline function). Finally, erase-remove trims the singleton words safely.

## Recap

- `std::vector<T>` is the go-to dynamic array; it manages its own memory and grows automatically.
- `std::string` is mutable and supports `+`/`+=` concatenation, unlike Python/Java strings; short strings often avoid heap allocation via SSO.
- `<algorithm>` provides `sort`, `find`, `remove_if`, `accumulate`, etc. that operate generically on iterator ranges.
- `operator[]` is fast but unchecked; `.at()` is checked and throws `std::out_of_range` — pick based on whether the index is trusted.
- The erase-remove idiom (`v.erase(remove_if(...), v.end())`) is the safe, idiomatic way to delete elements matching a condition.
- `map`/`set` give sorted, O(log n) associative storage; `unordered_map`/`unordered_set` trade order for average O(1).

codeExamples.code:
