A `class` bundles data (**members**) and the functions that operate on it (**methods**) into one unit.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Animal {
public:
    Animal(string n) : name(n) {}  // constructor, using a member initializer list
    string speak() { return name + " makes a sound"; }

private:
    string name;
};

int main() {
    Animal a("Generic Animal");
    cout << a.speak() << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

## Access Specifiers

- **`public`** — accessible from anywhere the object is visible.
- **`private`** — accessible only from inside the class's own methods (the default for `class`).
- **`protected`** — like `private`, but also accessible to derived classes.

Keeping data members `private` and exposing only the methods you intend callers to use is **encapsulation**: it lets you change the internal representation later without breaking code that uses the class, as long as the public interface stays the same.

## Constructors and the Member Initializer List

```cpp
Animal(string n) : name(n) {}
```

The `: name(n)` part is a **member initializer list** — it directly initializes `name` from the constructor's parameter, which is more efficient than assigning inside the constructor body (assignment would first default-construct `name`, then overwrite it). For members that are references or lack a default constructor, the initializer list isn't just faster — it's the *only* way to initialize them at all.

## Inheritance

```cpp
class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    Dog(string n) : Animal(n) {}  // must construct the base first
};
```

`: public Base` expresses public inheritance: `Dog` gets all of `Animal`'s public members as its own, plus whatever it adds. The base class's constructor always runs before the derived class's constructor body, which is why `Dog`'s constructor delegates to `Animal(n)` in its own initializer list.

## Static vs. Dynamic Dispatch

By default, C++ resolves which method to call **at compile time**, based on the *declared* type of the variable, not the actual object it holds:

```cpp
class Animal {
public:
    string speak() { return "..."; }  // NOT virtual
};
class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    string speak() { return "Woof!"; }
};

Animal* a = new Dog();
cout << a->speak() << endl; // calls Animal::speak() — "..." NOT "Woof!"
```

Marking the base method `virtual` switches to **dynamic dispatch**: the call is resolved at runtime based on the object's *actual* type.

```cpp
class Animal {
public:
    virtual string speak() { return "..."; }
};
class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    string speak() override { return "Woof!"; }
};

Animal* a = new Dog();
cout << a->speak() << endl; // "Woof!" — resolved at runtime
```

`override` (C++11+) isn't required for virtual dispatch to work, but you should always add it: it tells the compiler "I intend to override a virtual base method," and the compiler will *error* if no matching virtual function actually exists in the base — catching typos in the signature that would otherwise silently create an unrelated overload instead of an override.

## How This Works: the vtable

Each class with at least one `virtual` function gets a compiler-generated **virtual table (vtable)** — an array of function pointers, one per virtual function, shared by all instances of that class. Every object of such a class secretly carries one extra hidden pointer (the **vptr**) pointing to its class's vtable. A call through a base pointer/reference looks up the vptr, finds the actual class's vtable, and jumps to the correct override — that indirection is the (small, but nonzero) runtime cost of polymorphism.

## Construction and Destruction Order

Base subobjects are constructed **before** the derived class's own constructor body runs, and destroyed in the **reverse** order — derived destructor body first, then derived members, then the base:

```cpp
class Base { public: Base() { cout << "Base ctor\n"; } ~Base() { cout << "Base dtor\n"; } };
class Derived : public Base { public: Derived() { cout << "Derived ctor\n"; } ~Derived() { cout << "Derived dtor\n"; } };
// new Derived() then delete: Base ctor, Derived ctor, Derived dtor, Base dtor
```

This mirrors intuition: you can't safely build the specialized "Derived" parts until the general "Base" foundation exists, and you must tear down the specialized parts before the foundation is removed.

## Object Slicing

Assigning a derived object into a base object **by value** truncates ("slices off") the derived-only parts:

```cpp
Dog d("Rex");
Animal a = d;      // SLICED: a is just an Animal, Dog's extra data/behavior is gone
cout << a.speak() << endl; // calls Animal::speak(), even if speak() is virtual!
```

Because `a` is a genuine `Animal` object (not a `Dog` in disguise), virtual dispatch has nothing to dispatch to — the `Dog`-specific override simply isn't part of `a`. Slicing is why polymorphism is almost always used through **pointers or references** (`Animal*`, `Animal&`), never through plain base-type value variables or by-value parameters/returns of a base type.

## Always Give Polymorphic Base Classes a Virtual Destructor

```cpp
class Animal {
public:
    virtual ~Animal() {}   // virtual destructor
    virtual string speak() { return "..."; }
};
```

If a base class's destructor is **not** virtual and you `delete` a derived object through a base pointer, only the base's destructor runs — the derived part's destructor (and therefore any cleanup it does, like freeing its own heap allocations) is skipped entirely. This is a serious, common source of resource leaks:

```cpp
Animal* a = new Dog("Rex"); // Dog might own extra heap resources
delete a; // if ~Animal() isn't virtual, ~Dog() never runs -> leak
```

**Rule of thumb: if a class has any `virtual` function, give it a `virtual` destructor too** — even if empty — the moment you plan to delete derived objects through a base pointer.

## Abstract Classes via Pure Virtual Functions

A function declared with `= 0` is a **pure virtual function**: it has no implementation in the base and forces every concrete derived class to provide one. A class with at least one pure virtual function is **abstract** — it cannot be instantiated directly:

```cpp
class Shape {
public:
    virtual double area() const = 0;   // pure virtual
    virtual ~Shape() {}
};

class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    Circle(double r) : radius(r) {}
    double area() const override { return 3.14159 * radius * radius; }
private:
    double radius;
};

// Shape s;       // ERROR: cannot instantiate an abstract class
Shape* s = new Circle(2.0); // fine — Circle provides area()
```

Abstract classes are how C++ expresses "here is an interface every subtype must implement" — conceptually similar to an `interface` in Java or a protocol in Swift, just expressed through pure virtual functions rather than a dedicated keyword.

This example ties together encapsulation, constructors, virtual dispatch, a virtual destructor, and an abstract base class.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Shape {
public:
    Shape(string n) : name(n) {}
    virtual ~Shape() {}                          // virtual destructor: mandatory here
    virtual double area() const = 0;              // pure virtual: makes Shape abstract
    string describe() const {
        return name + " has area " + to_string(area());
    }
protected:
    string name;                                   // visible to derived classes
};

class Rectangle : public Shape {
public:
    Rectangle(double w, double h) : Shape("Rectangle"), width(w), height(h) {}
    double area() const override { return width * height; }
private:
    double width, height;
};

class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    Circle(double r) : Shape("Circle"), radius(r) {}
    double area() const override { return 3.14159 * radius * radius; }
private:
    double radius;
};

int main() {
    // unique_ptr<Shape> lets us hold different concrete types via one base pointer,
    // with automatic cleanup (no manual delete, no leaked derived destructors).
    vector<unique_ptr<Shape>> shapes;
    shapes.push_back(make_unique<Rectangle>(3.0, 4.0));
    shapes.push_back(make_unique<Circle>(2.0));

    for (const auto& s : shapes) {
        cout << s->describe() << endl; // virtual dispatch picks the right area()
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Every piece from this module shows up here: `Shape` is abstract because `area()` is pure virtual, so it can only ever be used through pointers to concrete derived types. `Shape`'s destructor is `virtual`, so destroying a `Rectangle` or `Circle` through a `unique_ptr<Shape>` correctly runs the derived destructor first. `describe()` is a regular (non-virtual) method defined once in the base and inherited unchanged by both derived classes, while `area()` is overridden differently by each — a clean split between shared and specialized behavior. Note `unique_ptr` (from the Pointers module) rather than raw `new`/`delete` — combining polymorphism with RAII smart pointers is the idiomatic modern C++ way to manage a collection of heap-allocated polymorphic objects.

## Recap

- `: public Base` expresses public inheritance; access specifiers (`public`/`private`/`protected`) control encapsulation.
- `virtual` + `override` enables runtime polymorphism via a compiler-generated vtable; without `virtual`, dispatch is static (resolved by declared type).
- Base subobjects construct before, and destruct after, the derived class's own construction/destruction.
- Assigning a derived object into a base-type value variable causes object slicing — polymorphism requires pointers or references.
- Any class with a virtual function should have a virtual destructor, or deleting through a base pointer skips derived cleanup.
- A pure virtual function (`= 0`) makes a class abstract, forcing derived classes to implement it — C++'s equivalent of an interface.

