## Null Pointers

A pointer that points to nothing should be set to `nullptr` (C++11+; avoid the old `NULL` or plain `0` in new code):

```cpp
int* p = nullptr;
if (p != nullptr) {
    cout << *p << endl; // never runs; safely guarded
}
```

Dereferencing a null pointer (`*p` when `p == nullptr`) is undefined behavior — typically a crash (segmentation fault) on most platforms, but the standard doesn't guarantee that; always check before dereferencing a pointer that might be null.

## Dangling Pointers

A pointer becomes **dangling** when it still holds an address, but the memory at that address is no longer valid — the object there was destroyed or freed:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int* danger() {
    int local = 5;
    return &local;   // returns address of a variable about to be destroyed
}                    // local's stack frame is popped here

int main() {
    int* p = danger();
    cout << *p << endl; // undefined behavior: reading freed stack memory
    return 0;
}
```

The same problem happens on the heap after a `delete`:

```cpp
int* p = new int(5);
delete p;
cout << *p << endl;  // undefined behavior: use-after-free
p = nullptr;          // good practice: prevents accidental reuse
```

Setting a pointer to `nullptr` immediately after `delete`-ing it is a cheap habit that turns a silent use-after-free into a loud, checkable null-dereference if the bug is triggered again.

## Memory Leaks in Practice

Leaks are especially easy to introduce via early returns or exceptions that skip a `delete`:

```cpp
void process(bool fail) {
    int* buffer = new int[100];
    if (fail) return;      // LEAK: buffer never freed on this path
    // ... use buffer ...
    delete[] buffer;
}
```

Every additional exit path (`return`, `throw`, `break` out of a loop holding a resource) is another place a manual `delete` can be forgotten. This is exactly the class of bug that motivates the next page's solution: never let a resource's lifetime depend on remembering to write the matching cleanup call at every possible exit point.

This example contrasts a leak-prone raw-pointer version with a `unique_ptr`-based version of a simple owning class, showing the Rule of Five in action versus letting a smart pointer do the work.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

// Version A: manual ownership. Needs Rule of Five to be correct;
// here we deliberately show what's required.
class Buffer {
public:
    explicit Buffer(size_t n) : size(n), data(new int[n]{}) {
        cout << "allocated " << size << " ints" << endl;
    }
    ~Buffer() { delete[] data; cout << "freed buffer" << endl; }

    // Rule of Five: without these, the compiler-generated copy would
    // copy the pointer, not the data -> double free on destruction.
    Buffer(const Buffer& other) : size(other.size), data(new int[other.size]) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < size; i++) data[i] = other.data[i];
    }
    Buffer& operator=(const Buffer& other) {
        if (this == &other) return *this;
        delete[] data;
        size = other.size;
        data = new int[size];
        for (size_t i = 0; i < size; i++) data[i] = other.data[i];
        return *this;
    }
    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept : size(other.size), data(other.data) {
        other.data = nullptr; other.size = 0;
    }
    Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& other) noexcept {
        if (this == &other) return *this;
        delete[] data;
        size = other.size; data = other.data;
        other.data = nullptr; other.size = 0;
        return *this;
    }

    size_t size;
    int* data;
};

// Version B: let unique_ptr's own Rule-of-Five implementation do the work.
class SimpleOwner {
public:
    explicit SimpleOwner(size_t n) : data(make_unique<int[]>(n)) {}
    // No destructor, copy/move ctor/assignment needed at all —
    // unique_ptr is move-only and non-copyable by default, which is
    // usually exactly the right default for an owning member.
private:
    unique_ptr<int[]> data;
};

int main() {
    Buffer b1(5);
    Buffer b2 = b1;        // deep copy, thanks to the copy constructor above
    Buffer b3 = move(b1);  // ownership transferred; b1 is now empty

    SimpleOwner owner(10); // no manual lifetime code needed anywhere
    cout << "done" << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

`Buffer` shows the full Rule of Five that a raw owning pointer demands: without the copy constructor and copy assignment operator, the compiler-generated defaults would copy the `int*` value itself (both objects pointing at the same heap block), and when both destructors ran, the second `delete[]` would be a double-free. `SimpleOwner` shows the payoff of preferring `unique_ptr<int[]>` as the member instead: it needs *none* of that boilerplate, because `unique_ptr` already correctly implements move-only ownership — this is why modern C++ guidance is to reach for smart pointers before hand-rolling ownership logic.

## Recap

- A pointer stores an address; `&` takes an address, `*` dereferences a pointer.
- Local variables live on the stack (automatic lifetime); `new` allocates on the heap (manual lifetime) and must be paired with exactly one `delete`/`delete[]`.
- Dereferencing a null or dangling pointer, or double-freeing memory, is undefined behavior.
- RAII ties resource cleanup to object lifetime, making cleanup automatic on every exit path, including exceptions.
- `unique_ptr` gives sole, move-only ownership; `shared_ptr` gives reference-counted shared ownership; `weak_ptr` observes without owning, breaking reference cycles.
- Prefer smart pointers (RAII) over raw `new`/`delete` in new code — they eliminate the need to hand-write the Rule of Three/Five in most classes.

