`<fstream>` extends the stream abstraction from `<iostream>` to files: `ofstream` for writing, `ifstream` for reading, and `fstream` for both.

## Writing a File

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ofstream out("data.txt");   // opens (creates/truncates) the file for writing
    if (!out) {                  // check the stream converts to bool: did the open succeed?
        cerr << "Could not open file for writing" << endl;
        return 1;
    }
    out << "hello" << endl;
    out << "world" << endl;
    return 0; // out's destructor closes the file automatically here (RAII)
}
```

## Reading a File

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ifstream in("data.txt");
    if (!in) {
        cerr << "Could not open file for reading" << endl;
        return 1;
    }
    string line;
    while (getline(in, line)) {   // getline returns the stream; false when input ends
        cout << line << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

`getline(in, line)` inside a `while` condition works because `istream` overloads conversion to `bool`: the loop keeps running as long as the read succeeded, and stops cleanly at end-of-file with no separate "am I done?" check needed.

## Checking Success, Not Just Hoping

Always check `if (!out)` / `if (!in)` after opening a file — a missing directory, a permissions problem, or a full disk will cause the open (or a later write) to fail, and silently ignoring that means your program appears to "work" while quietly losing data. `<fstream>` streams convert to `bool` (`true` if the stream is in a good state), which is what makes `if (!out)` and `while (getline(...))` both work naturally.

## RAII Closes Files Automatically

Just like the smart pointers and stack-unwinding examples in earlier modules, file streams follow RAII: their destructor calls `close()` automatically when they go out of scope, whether that's normal function exit or stack unwinding from an exception. Calling `.close()` explicitly is allowed and sometimes useful (e.g., to release the file before more code runs later in the same scope), but is not required for correctness.

## Open Modes

Both `ifstream`/`ofstream` accept a second argument controlling how the file is opened:

```cpp
#include <fstream>
ofstream out1("log.txt", ios::app);          // append, don't overwrite
ofstream out2("data.bin", ios::binary);       // binary mode, no text translation
fstream io("data.txt", ios::in | ios::out);   // both read and write
```

By default, `ofstream` **truncates** (erases existing content) when opening a file that already exists; `ios::app` instead appends every write to the end, which is the common choice for log files. `ios::binary` disables platform-specific text transformations (like `\n` ↔ `\r\n` translation on some historical platforms) so raw bytes round-trip exactly — essential when writing non-text data like serialized structs or images.

## Reading and Writing Binary Data

```cpp
struct Point { double x, y; };

Point p{3.5, 7.2};
ofstream out("point.bin", ios::binary);
out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&p), sizeof(p));

Point loaded;
ifstream in("point.bin", ios::binary);
in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&loaded), sizeof(loaded));
```

`.write()`/`.read()` operate on raw bytes rather than formatted text, which is why the pointer must be reinterpreted as `const char*`/`char*` — the stream doesn't know or care about `Point`'s actual layout, it just copies `sizeof(p)` bytes. This is fast but fragile: it assumes the exact same struct layout (padding, byte order) when reading back, which only holds reliably within the same compiler/platform/build.

## stringstream: Streams Backed by a String

`<sstream>` provides `istringstream`/`ostringstream`/`stringstream`, which use the same `<<`/`>>` interface as `cin`/`cout` but read from or write to an in-memory `string` instead of the console or a file:

```cpp
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

string csv = "42,foo,3.14";
stringstream ss(csv);
string token;
while (getline(ss, token, ',')) {  // getline's 3rd arg: custom delimiter
    cout << token << endl;
}

ostringstream oss;
oss << "Score: " << 95 << "/" << 100;
string message = oss.str();  // "Score: 95/100"
```

This is the idiomatic C++ way to parse delimited text or build a formatted string piece by piece without manual string concatenation — notice `getline` here takes a custom delimiter (`','`) instead of the default `'\n'`, splitting on commas instead of lines.

The STL is more than containers — three headers in particular round out everyday C++ development.

## `<algorithm>`: Generic Operations on Ranges

Already introduced in the Arrays/STL module, `<algorithm>` covers far more than `sort`:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
vector<int> v = {5, 3, 8, 1, 9};

sort(v.begin(), v.end());                          // ascending sort
bool has8 = find(v.begin(), v.end(), 8) != v.end(); // linear search
int biggest = *max_element(v.begin(), v.end());     // largest element
transform(v.begin(), v.end(), v.begin(),
          [](int n) { return n * n; });              // square every element in place
```

`transform` applies a function to every element of a range and writes the results into a (possibly different) destination range — here, writing back into `v` itself squares every element in place.

## `<numeric>`: Numeric Reductions

```cpp
#include <numeric>
vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int sum = accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0);          // 15
int product = accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 1,
                          [](int a, int b) { return a * b; }); // 120
```

`accumulate` folds a range down to a single value using an operation (addition by default, or a custom lambda) — the same general pattern as `reduce`/`fold` in other languages.

## `<chrono>`: Type-Safe Time

```cpp
#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
using namespace std::chrono;

auto start = high_resolution_clock::now();
// ... work to time ...
auto end = high_resolution_clock::now();
auto elapsed = duration_cast<milliseconds>(end - start);
cout << elapsed.count() << " ms" << endl;
```

`<chrono>` represents durations and time points with their *units* baked into the type system (`milliseconds`, `seconds`, `microseconds`), so the compiler catches unit-mismatch mistakes (like accidentally treating milliseconds as seconds) that would otherwise be a silent runtime bug in a plain-integer timing API.

## Choosing the Right Tool

A rough rule of thumb across everything covered in this course: reach for `vector`/`string`/`map` for storage, `<algorithm>`/`<numeric>` for processing that storage, `<fstream>`/`<sstream>` for text and binary I/O, and `<chrono>` whenever "how long did that take" or "what time is it" comes up. Very little application-level C++ code needs to drop below these tools to raw loops and manual pointer arithmetic — and when it does, that's usually a deliberate, justified performance decision rather than the default starting point.

This closing example reads a CSV-style log file, parses it with `stringstream`, aggregates with `<numeric>`, and times the whole operation with `<chrono>` — a realistic combination of everything in this module.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <sstream>
#include <vector>
#include <numeric>
#include <algorithm>
#include <chrono>
#include <string>
using namespace std;
using namespace std::chrono;

struct Reading { string sensor; double value; };

int main() {
    // Write a small sample log first, so this example is self-contained.
    {
        ofstream out("readings.csv");
        out << "tempA,21.5\n";
        out << "tempB,19.8\n";
        out << "tempA,22.1\n";
        out << "tempB,20.4\n";
    } // ofstream closes here automatically (RAII)

    auto start = high_resolution_clock::now();

    ifstream in("readings.csv");
    if (!in) {
        cerr << "Could not open readings.csv" << endl;
        return 1;
    }

    vector<Reading> readings;
    string line;
    while (getline(in, line)) {
        stringstream ss(line);
        string sensor, valueStr;
        getline(ss, sensor, ',');
        getline(ss, valueStr, ',');
        readings.push_back({sensor, stod(valueStr)});
    }

    vector<double> tempAValues;
    for (const auto& r : readings) {
        if (r.sensor == "tempA") tempAValues.push_back(r.value);
    }

    double total = accumulate(tempAValues.begin(), tempAValues.end(), 0.0);
    double average = tempAValues.empty() ? 0.0 : total / tempAValues.size();
    double highest = *max_element(tempAValues.begin(), tempAValues.end());

    auto end = high_resolution_clock::now();
    auto elapsed = duration_cast<microseconds>(end - start);

    cout << "tempA average: " << average << endl;
    cout << "tempA highest: " << highest << endl;
    cout << "Processed " << readings.size() << " readings in "
         << elapsed.count() << " microseconds" << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

The nested block around the initial `ofstream out(...)` deliberately closes it (via RAII, when the block ends) before reading begins, avoiding any ambiguity about whether the writes are flushed. Each line is parsed with a `stringstream` split on commas via `getline(ss, field, ',')`, mirroring the delimiter-splitting technique from the previous page. `accumulate` and `max_element` from `<numeric>`/`<algorithm>` compute the aggregate stats without a hand-written loop, and `<chrono>`'s `high_resolution_clock` brackets the whole parse-and-aggregate step to report timing in a self-documenting unit (`microseconds`).

## Recap

- `ifstream` reads, `ofstream` writes, `fstream` does both; all three live in `<fstream>` and follow RAII, closing automatically when they go out of scope.
- Always check a file stream's success (`if (!stream)`) after opening — silent failures lose data.
- `ios::app` appends instead of truncating; `ios::binary` disables text-mode translation for exact byte round-tripping.
- `stringstream`/`istringstream`/`ostringstream` (`<sstream>`) apply the familiar `<<`/`>>` interface to in-memory strings — ideal for parsing delimited text.
- `<algorithm>` and `<numeric>` provide generic, well-tested operations (`sort`, `transform`, `accumulate`, `max_element`) that work across any container.
- `<chrono>` represents durations and time points with their units baked into the type system, avoiding unit-confusion bugs in timing code.

